1. Acceptance

By exercising any rights granted under this License, you accept and agree to comply with all terms and conditions of this License.

If you do not agree to these terms, you may not use, copy, modify, distribute, deploy, or otherwise exercise rights in the Software.

2. Definitions

For purposes of this License, the following definitions apply.

2.1 “Software”

“Software” means the Bridge365 source code, object code, configuration files, scripts, documentation, templates, schemas, prompts, workflows, and other materials explicitly distributed by the Licensor under this License.

The Software does not include third-party components that are separately identified as being subject to other license terms.

2.2 “Licensor”

“Licensor” means the individual or legal entity that owns the copyright in the Software and makes the Software available under this License.

2.3 “Licensee” and “You”

“Licensee” and “You” mean the individual or legal entity exercising rights under this License.

If you exercise rights on behalf of a legal entity, “You” includes that legal entity.

2.4 “Affiliate”

“Affiliate” means a legal entity that directly or indirectly controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with the Licensee.

For this purpose, “control” means ownership of more than 50 percent of the voting interests of the relevant legal entity or the legal power to direct its management.

2.5 “Derivative Work”

“Derivative Work” means any modification, enhancement, adaptation, translation, extension, fork, port, or other work based on or incorporating a substantial portion of the Software.

A separate program that merely communicates with the Software through documented interfaces, APIs, protocols, connectors, command-line interfaces, or extension mechanisms is not a Derivative Work solely because of that communication.

2.6 “Internal Business Use”

“Internal Business Use” means use of the Software by the Licensee or its Affiliates in support of their own operations, employees, customers, products, or business processes.

Internal Business Use may include production deployment and the processing of data or communications belonging to the Licensee, its Affiliates, or their customers, provided that customers are not offered access to the Software as a prohibited Hosted Service.

2.7 “Customer-Specific Deployment”

“Customer-Specific Deployment” means a separate instance of the Software that:

is configured or operated for one identified customer;
is logically separated from installations used by other customers;
is used primarily for that customer’s own business processes;
is not marketed or licensed as a standalone Bridge365 product; and
does not provide multiple unrelated customers with access to a shared Bridge365 service.

A Customer-Specific Deployment may be hosted in the customer’s environment or operated by a service provider on the customer’s behalf.

2.8 “Hosted Service”

“Hosted Service” means a service through which a third party is given direct or indirect access to a substantial set of the Software’s features or functionality, whether through a user interface, API, application, agent, embedded component, or other technical mechanism.

2.9 “Competing Service”

“Competing Service” means a Hosted Service, SaaS offering, platform, managed service, or commercially distributed product where:

the Software or a Derivative Work provides a substantial portion of the service’s functionality; and
access to that functionality is a primary or substantial part of the value offered to customers.

A Customer-Specific Deployment provided as part of professional services is not a Competing Service if it satisfies Section 2.7 and the customer is not primarily purchasing access to Bridge365 itself.

2.10 “Sell” or “Sale”

“Sell” or “Sale” means receiving payment or other compensation primarily in exchange for:

access to the Software;
distribution of the Software;
a license or sublicense to the Software;
access to a Derivative Work;
distribution of a Derivative Work; or
a license or sublicense to a Derivative Work.

Payment for professional services does not constitute a Sale of the Software if the primary value of the transaction is consulting, implementation, customization, integration, training, maintenance, support, or operation services rather than access to or redistribution of the Software itself.

2.11 “Contribution”

“Contribution” means any original work of authorship, modification, correction, documentation, or other material intentionally submitted to the Licensor for inclusion in the Software.

A Contribution does not include material clearly marked by the contributor as “Not a Contribution” or similar wording.

2.12 “Contributor”

“Contributor” means an individual or legal entity that submits a Contribution to the Licensor.

